## BUG-01 (MEDIUM): eBPF probe uses wrong path (missing node_id) — corrected
# Files: enforcer/PolicyEnforcer.cpp:184, 302, cell/NodeAgent.cpp:122-125
#
# Maps DO match (both xdp_blacklist). Pipeline is consistent:
#   PolicyEnforcer writes → xdp_blacklist → sensor.bpf.c reads from it
#   NodeAgent pins map at /sys/fs/bpf/neuro_mesh_<node_id>/xdp_blacklist
#
# BUT: ensure_ebpf_map() at line 184 probes /sys/fs/bpf/neuro_mesh/xdp_blacklist
# (no node_id), while apply_ebpf_drop() at line 302 uses the correct path
# WITH node_id. Probe always fails → available_backends() never reports EBPF.
#
# However, isolate_target() and block_ip_address() call apply_ebpf_drop()
# UNCONDITIONALLY (before checking backends), so enforcement still works.
# The bug is broken diagnostics: always shows "eBPF: unavailable" even when
# eBPF enforcement is succeeding. neuro_bpf.c is dead code (uncompiled).
#
# Fix: Add node_id to the probe path in ensure_ebpf_map(), or remove it and
# rely on the unconditional try. Remove neuro_bpf.c if unused.

## BUG-02 (MEDIUM): block_ip_address() lacks internal safe-list guard — latent
# Files: enforcer/PolicyEnforcer.cpp:513-556, enforcer/MitigationEngine.cpp:254-269
#
# block_ip_address() checks is_valid_ip() and is_loopback() only. Has NO
# internal is_safe() or is_ip_safe() call. The ONLY caller (MitigationEngine::
# execute_response()) DOES check is_ip_safe() at line 254-257 before calling,
# so the current code path is protected.
#
# Latent vulnerability: any future code path that calls block_ip_address()
# directly without a prior safe-list check will bypass the safe list. In
# contrast, isolate_target() has its own defensive is_safe() check at line 578.
#
# Fix: Add is_ip_safe() guard inside block_ip_address() for defense-in-depth.

